3.3.m:
Kṛṣṇa said:
Homage to the peaceful Rudra, the lord of spirits, the matted-haired one;
homage, homage to the maker of Time, the sustainer of the world, the destroyer of sin!

Restored to life again, they slew those commanders of the opposing army.
Tālan and the other heroes became distressed there;
despairing of victory, they took refuge with the incarnation of Kṛṣṇa.

Bowing to the queen, endowed with every beauty, who stood upon the mansion,
he spoke gentle words: "I am your servant Udaya here;
I have come to you for refuge, O fair lady, as if to Kāmākṣī.

Hearing his weeping, the wicked king there
went quickly with a cheerful heart to where Āhlāda himself was staying;
weeping bitterly there, humble in mind, he spoke these words:

His mother, wife, and sister, distressed with love,
tried to enlighten Āhlāda, who was deluded by the rogue's illusion;
[yet] the warrior, deluded by the wondrous illusion, could not be enlightened then.

By command of Jagannātha he arrived, eager for battle with them;
and between the two there occurred a great, tumultuous combat that caused the hair to stand on end.
In the span of a day and a night, the king was conquered by Kṛṣṇāṃśa.

Those heroes, filled with great pride, went to the city of Vīrasiṃha;
they besieged the entire city situated atop the lofty snow-mountain,
which was protected by the yogin named Gorakha for the sake of his devotees.

Severing arrows with arrows, both devotees of the Goddess,
having mutually destroyed each other's mounts, came to the ground;
bearing swords and shields, the two heroes fought each other.

And Kṛṣṇāṃśa is known to the noble king as of low caste;
therefore his daughter was not sent to your wise son.
Therefore, respecting my word, do what is agreeable to you.

Hearing this, Malanā said to the wrathful king:
“O King, just as [he is my] kinsman, so indeed am I;
heed my word, O King, otherwise I shall renounce my life-breaths!”

“O illustrious Brahmānanda, listen attentively to our words:
Your wife Velā is herself Kālī, the embodiment of strife;
she has destroyed our husbands, and we are terribly aggrieved.

In the Kali [Yuga], indeed, a woman is vile, enjoying other men;
therefore, in the Kali age, the marriage of a widowed woman
was declared auspicious by Devala, and by Asita himself in his Smṛti.

Matta, Pramatta, Unmatta, Sumatta, and Durmada,
Durmukha, Durdhara, Bāhu, Suratha, and Viratha in order;
and their younger sister, whose name was well-famed, had intoxicating eyes.

Their great combat lasted for three watches;
and when evening arrived, the powerful Kṛṣṇāṃśa, weaponless,
rendered the enemy unconscious with a blow of his palm.

Then in Lakṣaṇa's army there arose a great cry of woe;
hearing their tumult and learning that the two were slain, King
Brahmānanda then threw off his faint and spoke to Velā:
